## 1. Project Name

**Sauce & Spoon Tabletop Menu Tablet Pilot**

## 2. Project Summary

Sauce & Spoon is a growing local restaurant chain with annual sales growth of 28%. That growth has not translated into profit: margins have stayed flat year over year. Order delivery times have risen by 20%, guest reviews increasingly cite "delay in service," and front-of-house turnover has reached 50%. Manual ticketing also leads to order errors, remakes, comped meals, and food waste.

This project will run a **pilot rollout of guest-facing tabletop menu tablets in the bar sections of two restaurants, Sauce & Spoon North and Sauce & Spoon Downtown.** The pilot launches in April and results will be evaluated at the end of June. Guests will be able to order right away, send specific requests and modifications straight to the kitchen, see promoted appetizers, specialty drinks, and coupons, and pay their own checks. The tablets will integrate with the existing point-of-sale (POS) and host software.

The pilot has three aims: serve more guests in less time (a 30-minute shorter table turn and 10% more daily guests), increase spend per visit (average check from $65 to at least $75, with more appetizer sales), and cut food waste by 25%. It will also track whether the kitchen can keep pace with higher demand, and it will give leadership the data to decide whether to expand tablets across all locations.

## 3. Project Goals

### Why stakeholders want this project
- Service is slowing as the company grows, and guests are noticing.
- Sales are up but profit margins are flat, so the company needs efficiency gains and a higher average check.
- Staff burnout and ordering errors are driving turnover, remakes, comps, and food waste.
- Leadership wants reliable data to guide growth and expansion decisions.
- It supports the company OKRs, including the commitment to *"innovate through annual projects (like menu tablets),"* dining experiences under an hour, a 4.5/5 average rating, and profit margins above 8%.

### What the project looks like when complete
Tablets are installed, integrated, and running in the bar sections of both pilot locations. Bar staff are trained and using them, guests are ordering and paying through them, and the appetizer, drink, and coupon promotions are live. Pilot data has been collected through the end of June and compared with baselines, kitchen capacity has been monitored, and leadership has a results report with a clear recommendation on expansion and kitchen staffing.

