**Notes on the goals**
- **Average check (goal 4):** The company-wide average check is $65 and Deanna wants at least $75. We may differentiate this by location once the GMs provide data. A simple supporting metric is the **number of guests who redeem the coupons** the tablets provide. Gilly also suggested specialty drinks, which are included alongside appetizers.
- **Guest wait time (removed as a separate goal):** The team agreed that a faster table turn already lowers guest wait time. When tables turn sooner, parties waiting for a seat are seated sooner, and quicker ordering and payment shortens the time guests wait for service once seated. Wait time also depends on how busy the restaurant is, so it will be **tracked as an indicator** rather than set as its own target. This change needs Deanna's confirmation (Appendix B, misalignment 5).
- **Kitchen satisfaction (not yet a goal):** Carter asked for a goal to improve kitchen staff satisfaction. It will be added only when Carter supplies specific metrics and a way to link it to the tablet launch (Appendix B, misalignment 2).
- **Order-return policy (not a goal):** Carter wants to stop replacing meals when a guest says an order was wrong. This policy change is handled separately by Deanna and Carter, not by this project (Appendix B, misalignment 1). The charter will be updated through change control if the policy changes.
- **Measurement window:** All goals are measured from the April launch through the end of June.

### SMART goal statements
The five goals with the biggest gaps were rewritten to be SMART (full analysis in *smart-goals-analysis.md*). **TBD** marks details still to be confirmed.

1. **Table turn time (goal 1):** Reduce the average table turn time in the bar sections of both locations from about 80 minutes to about 50 minutes (a 30-minute reduction) by June 30, by having guests order and pay at tabletop tablets. Turn time is measured from host software and compared with a baseline captured in the four weeks before launch (TBD).
2. **Appetizer sales (goal 3):** Increase appetizer revenue per guest in the bar sections by 10% at North and 20% at Downtown by June 30 (about 15% on average), compared with the pre-launch baseline (TBD), by showing appetizers and specialty drinks on the tablet menu and offering coupons. The share of parties ordering appetizers is tracked as a supporting metric.
3. **Average check (goal 4):** Raise the average check in the bar sections from the current baseline (company-wide $65; bar baseline TBD) to at least $75 by June 30, by promoting appetizers, specialty drinks, and coupons. Average check comes from monthly POS reports (April and May checkpoints TBD), with coupon redemption rate as a supporting metric (target TBD%).
4. **Food waste (goal 5):** Reduce food waste and comped meals in the bar sections by 25% by June 30, compared with a weekly baseline kept for four weeks before launch (TBD dollars per week per location), by sending guest orders and modifications straight to the kitchen. Measured weekly from the kitchen waste log and POS comp reports. A kitchen performance metric (how long guests wait for their food, from the kitchen's existing metrics; target TBD with Carter) is tracked alongside it.
5. **Kitchen capacity (goal 9):** By the April go-live, put in place a BOH capacity monitoring plan that tracks kitchen payroll hours and kitchen ticket times (trigger levels TBD) weekly at both locations, and by July 15 (date TBD) deliver a staffing recommendation to Deanna based on April to June data.

### How we will know the project is done
- Tablets are installed, configured, and live in the bar sections of both locations.
- POS and host integration is tested and working.
- All pilot-area staff have completed training.
- The pilot has run from April through the end of June and all metrics have been collected.
- The results report has been delivered to and accepted by Deanna.

### How we will know the project was successful
- Bar table turn time drops by about 30 minutes, with the 4.5/5 rating maintained.
- Daily guest counts rise by 10%.
- Appetizer sales rise by 10% at North and 20% at Downtown.
- Average check reaches at least $75.
- Food waste falls by 25%.
- The system runs reliably, with no major POS or host integration failures.
- Stakeholders have enough data to make an informed expand, adjust, or stop decision, including how to staff the kitchen.

## 4. Scope and Exclusions

### In scope
- Pilot of guest-facing tabletop menu tablets in the **bar section only** at Sauce & Spoon North and Sauce & Spoon Downtown (chosen because each location has the right staff size and guest volume for a pilot)
- Guests ordering on the tablet as soon as they are seated and **paying their own checks** at the table
- Research and selection of a tablet package with the **menu add-on and coupon features**, done with Seydou. Initial promotions focus on appetizers and specialty drinks
- Hardware and software implementation, and configuration and integration with the **existing** POS and host software, with account services support
- Digital menu layout for the tablets and the related website update, as priced in the vendor quote (confirm what is included)
- Sending guest requests and menu modifications directly to the kitchen, with a record of them
- Staff training plan, training materials, and training for pilot-area staff
- Vendor maintenance and IT support through the end of the year (as quoted)
- Estimates of how much food waste the project can cut, and kitchen food-wait metrics that show kitchen performance
- Data collection and tracking by location (turn time, guest counts, appetizer sales, average check, coupon redemption, waste and comps, kitchen food-wait time, guest feedback)
- Monitoring of back-of-house (BOH) payroll and kitchen bandwidth during the pilot
- Preparation in the quarter before launch, then the pilot from April through the end of June
- Pilot results report with an expansion recommendation and a kitchen staffing recommendation

### Out of scope (exclusions)
- Rollout to dining room sections or other restaurant locations
- **Replacing or buying a new POS or host system.** The tablets integrate with the existing systems (Appendix B, misalignment 6)
- **Changing the order-return policy** (replacing meals when guests report an incorrect order). Deanna and Carter will discuss it separately as an operations matter (Appendix B, misalignment 1)
- **A kitchen staff satisfaction goal**, unless Carter provides specific metrics (Appendix B, misalignment 2)
- **Hiring and payroll reallocation.** Decisions about moving front-of-house (FOH) payroll or hiring kitchen staff (line cooks, bussers, runners) wait for pilot data and Deanna's approval (Appendix B, misalignment 3)
- A separate guest wait-time target (Appendix B, misalignment 5)
- Changes to menu items, recipes, or prices (the digital layout of the existing menu is in scope)
- Promotions for specific entrees. The add-on feature supports them, but the pilot goals target appetizers and specialty drinks; confirm with Deanna
- Company-wide expansion (a future project, dependent on pilot results)

