### 6.2 Estimated benefits (12-week pilot, base case)

| Benefit | How it is estimated | Estimate |
|---|---|---|
| More guests served | +10% bar guests (8 more per night per location) at $28 average spend, 72 operating nights, 35% contribution margin | $11,290 |
| Higher spend per guest from appetizers, drinks, and coupons | +$1.50 per guest across 176 guests per night, 72 nights, 35% contribution margin | $6,653 |
| Less food waste | 25% reduction on $150 per week per location in comps and waste | $900 |
| **Total estimated pilot benefit** | | **$18,843** |

**Benefits that are not given a dollar value:**
- Faster service and shorter waits, which supports the 4.5/5 rating and the under-an-hour dining goal
- Fewer ordering errors, because guest requests and modifications are sent to the kitchen directly and recorded
- Less pressure on front-of-house staff, which may reduce burnout and turnover (currently 50%)
- Reliable data on turn time, product mix, and waste to guide growth and expansion decisions
- Better reviews and guest retention as delays in service shrink
- Keeping pace with guests' growing expectation of a digital dining experience
- A repeatable approach for the company's annual innovation projects

**Costs and trade-offs that are not given a dollar value:**
- Staff learning curve and possible resistance during the early weeks
- Some guests may prefer ordering from a server
- Time that managers spend on training and feedback
- Possible extra strain on the kitchen if tables turn faster before staffing is adjusted

### 6.3 Summary

| Measure | Base case | If the $75 average check goal is met |
|---|---|---|
| Total cost | $50,550 | $50,550 |
| Benefit during the 12-week pilot | $18,843 | $31,468 |
| Net position at the end of the pilot | -$31,707 | -$19,082 |
| Estimated payback point | About 32 weeks after go-live | About 19 weeks after go-live |
| 12-month view if results are sustained | Benefit $81,653, net about $31,100 (about 62% return) | Benefit $136,361, net about $85,800 (about 170% return) |

The pilot is not expected to pay for itself within its 12 weeks. The base case assumes a spend uplift of about $3.45 per check, while Deanna's goal is a **$10 increase per check ($65 to $75)**, so the average check goal is the biggest driver of the return. These figures do not include fees after the end of the year, which are not quoted. The pilot's main value is proving the benefit at limited cost before a company-wide rollout.

